<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02026</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1c677028-d6db-4570-a042-4717aaf6b0af</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Expressions in different forms can be used to define the same function. Here are three ways to define a function
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Vertex form</emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=x^2-4x+3\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-2)^2-1\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=ax^2+bx+c\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<para id="para-00002">Which form would you use if you want to find the following features of the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>? Be prepared to explain
  your reasoning.</para>
<section id="sect-00002"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise id="_9cf689e7-5f8c-4a48-ba74-2a2f83303671" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00003">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00004">Correct! Factored form. In factored form, we can see that the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts, or roots, are located at
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para><para id="para-00005">Incorrect. The correct answer is factored form. In factored form, we can see that the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts, or
        roots, are located at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_380e1643-a11c-4cb6-8845-ec65a4da1a50" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00006">The vertex</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00007">Correct! Vertex form. In vertex form, we can see that the graph has a vertex located at the point defined by
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para><para id="para-00008">Incorrect. The correct answer is vertex form. In vertex form, we can see that the graph has a vertex located at
        the point defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_6e893041-f82f-43ab-a110-2237251bfb5d" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00009">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00010">Correct! In standard form, we can see that the graph intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at 3, but in any form, we can
        pretty easily find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept when the input is 0.</para><para id="para-00011">Incorrect. The correct answer is standard form. In standard form, we can see that the graph intersects the
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at 3, but in any form, we can pretty easily find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept when the input is 0.</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content></document>
